Ladybird ends the automatic height of a block formatting context root at
the bottom margin edge of its last in-flow child, with the margins that
collapse there, and then extends it to the floats inside it. CSS 2 names
the “bottommost” child there instead, and a search of the csswg-drafts
issues turned up none about that wording.

Blink’s BlockLayoutAlgorithm::FinishLayout() starts from the block
offset after the last in-flow child, adds the sum of the end margin
strut for a new formatting context, and extends the size to what
ExclusionSpace::ClearanceOffsetIncludingInitialLetter() answers.
WebKit’s RenderBlockFlow::layoutBlockChild() sets the logical height
of the block past each child in turn, so it ends up past the last
one. Gecko’s nsBlockFrame::ComputeFinalSize() takes mBCoord, the block
coordinate that the reflow state reached, plus the margin that isn’t
carried out of the block.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5, the test’s
27 cases come out the same.
